---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro'
import SlackThread from '../../components/slack/SlackThread.astro'
import { firstChartSeries, slackDemo as demo } from '../../lib/slack-fixtures'

const title = 'Slack alerts, message by message'
const description =
  'One disk alert from first warning to resolved, as chmonitor sends it to Slack through an incoming webhook and through a bot token. Every message is the real Block Kit JSON, drawn as HTML.'
const GUIDE = '/slack-alerts-threads-reactions/'
const { webhook, newAlert, escalation, ack, resolved } = demo.threads
const readings = firstChartSeries(escalation.replies[escalation.replies.length - 1])
---
<Base title={`${title} — chmonitor blog`} description={description}>
  <Nav />
  <main>
    <article class="slack-demo">
      <header class="slack-demo-head">
        <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Demo</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 text-balance font-normal text-[clamp(1.5rem,4.4vw,2.75rem)] leading-[1.06] tracking-tight text-foreground">
          {title}
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>
        <p class="slack-demo-intro">
          The alert is on a fake host, prod-ch-1.
          Its worst disk fills from {readings[0]}% to {Math.max(...readings)}%, a teammate picks it up, and it clears. A test rebuilds the JSON for
          this page from chmonitor's alert code, so the page cannot show a message
          chmonitor no longer sends. To turn this on for your own Slack, read <a href={GUIDE}>the setup guide</a>.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <div class="slack-demo-text">
          <h2>Webhook or bot token</h2>
          <p>
            An incoming webhook can only post. Every alert is a new message in the
            channel. A bot token lets chmonitor keep one standing message per
            route that counts what is open, and put the details in its thread.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="slack-demo-pair">
          <SlackThread
            thread={webhook}
            demo={demo}
            caption="Incoming webhook. A new message for every alert, warning, critical and resolved alike."
          />
          <SlackThread
            thread={newAlert}
            demo={demo}
            collapsed
            caption="Bot token. One message for the route, posted under the bot name and icon you set."
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="slack-demo-text">
        <h2>A new alert</h2>
        <p>
          The standing message says one warning is open. The thread reply has the
          rest: the value against both thresholds, what was measured, a chart of
          recent readings and an Acknowledge button. The runbook, rule id, team,
          region and time sit in a small gray footer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The chart is Slack's own chart block, <code>data_visualization</code>. Slack draws it
          in the message itself, no image upload. This page draws the same block
          JSON as SVG, so the look is ours. Series named warn and crit are dashed
          here, and Slack draws them like any other series. Slack's reference does not say
          whether incoming webhooks or older app installs can post the block, and
          we have not yet tried it against a live workspace. The webhook message
          has no chart.
        </p>
        <SlackThread thread={newAlert} demo={demo} caption="The first warning, opened in its thread." />
      </section>

      <section class="slack-demo-text">
        <h2>It gets worse</h2>
        <p>
          When the disk passes the critical threshold, the standing message is
          edited in place and turns red. The new details go in the same thread, so
          the channel gets no second message.
        </p>
        <SlackThread thread={escalation} demo={demo} caption="The same thread after the alert turns critical." />
      </section>

      <section class="slack-demo-text">
        <h2>Someone is on it</h2>
        <p>
          When a teammate clicks Acknowledge, the button becomes a line that names
          them and the bot adds 👀 to the alert. Anyone who opens the thread can
          see that the alert has an owner.
        </p>
        <SlackThread thread={ack} demo={demo} caption="The critical alert after it was acknowledged." />
      </section>

      <section class="slack-demo-text">
        <h2>Resolved</h2>
        <p>
          When the disk drops back, the standing message turns green and reads All
          clear. The thread gets a resolved reply that says how long the alert
          lasted and its peak, and quotes the alert as it first fired. The bot
          reacts ✅ on that first alert. The next incident starts a new standing
          message.
        </p>
        <SlackThread thread={resolved} demo={demo} caption="The whole incident in one thread." />
      </section>

      <section class="slack-demo-text">
        <h2>Set it up</h2>
        <p>
          The bot needs a Slack app with the chat:write, chat:write.customize and
          reactions:write scopes, its token in SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, and a channel id
          on the alert route. <a href={GUIDE}>The setup guide</a> walks through
          it. The reference is the <a href="https://docs.chmonitor.dev/guide/guides/alerting-slack-discord#slack-bot-one-standing-message-changes-in-its-thread">alerting docs</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .slack-demo {
    max-width: var(--maxw);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 56px 24px 96px;
  }
  .slack-demo section {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }
  .slack-demo-head,
  .slack-demo-text {
    max-width: var(--maxw-prose);
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  .slack-demo-text > :global(* + *) {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .slack-demo h2 {
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 26px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .slack-demo-intro,
  .slack-demo-text > p {
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }
  .slack-demo-intro {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .slack-demo-intro a,
  .slack-demo-text > p a {
    color: var(--brand-ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .slack-demo-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 400px), 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: start;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
</style>
